# Elixir's formatter always writes LF; letting Windows checkouts convert
# .ex/.exs to CRLF (via core.autocrlf) makes `mix format --check-formatted`
# fail on files nobody actually edited. Force LF for source Git tracks,
# regardless of local autocrlf settings. See docs/issues/ISS-0008.yaml.
*.ex text eol=lf
*.exs text eol=lf

# ISS-0210/GH#402: handoffs/orchestrator.log and handoffs/registry.json are both
# append-only by protocol (ORCHESTRATOR.md §7.1) and both conflict on nearly every
# concurrent run. Decided per-file rather than as a pair:
#
# orchestrator.log: ADOPTED. It is a flat line-oriented log; git's built-in `union`
# merge driver unions both sides' lines and drops exact duplicates, which is the same
# outcome hand-resolution already produces for this file (see the WF03-ISS0200-20260821
# git_evidence: line count only ever grew, origin/main's lines an exact prefix of the
# resolved file). Still re-validate the line count before/after per ORCHESTRATOR.md
# §7.1 even with this driver enabled — it changes who performs the union, not the
# obligation to check it landed correctly.
handoffs/orchestrator.log merge=union

# registry.json: NOT ADOPTED, deliberately. `union` unions LINES with no knowledge of
# JSON structure. Two concurrent sessions each appending one run-entry object commonly
# edit the SAME closing lines (the enclosing array's trailing `]` and the preceding
# entry's trailing `}`), which a naive line union can duplicate, drop, or interleave
# into syntactically invalid or duplicate-keyed JSON — exactly the failure mode this
# file cannot afford, since every run depends on it parsing. Hand-resolution (read both
# `<<<<<<<`/`=======`/`>>>>>>>` sides, compose by appending the missing entries in
# `created_at` order, re-validate with a JSON parser) stays mandatory for this file per
# ORCHESTRATOR.md §7.1. No merge attribute is set for it here.

# The frontend (web/, migrated 2026-08-21) is the same problem ISS-0008 recorded for
# Elixir sources, on a different toolchain: these files are authored and verified as
# LF, and letting core.autocrlf convert them per-checkout makes diffs between hosts
# noise rather than signal. ESLint, tsc, vitest and the guard scanners are all
# line-oriented; pinning LF keeps what one host verified identical to what the next
# one checks out. Kept as an explicit list rather than a blanket `* text=auto` so it
# changes nothing outside web/'s file types.
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must stay LF or bash fails on CRLF checkouts (scripts/uat_preflight.sh).
scripts/*.sh text eol=lf
scripts/lib/*.sh text eol=lf
# The QA redeploy script and its sourced helper run on the Linux host (GH 2352).
deploy/*.sh text eol=lf
deploy/lib/*.sh text eol=lf

# GH #2586 / REQ-498: the effective-permission golden file is compared line by line
# (`mix letflow.permission_matrix --check`) and read from git at the base commit; the
# writer always emits LF, so pin LF on every checkout (the parser also strips a
# trailing CR, but a CRLF working copy would still show as a whole-file diff).
priv/permission_matrix/*.golden text eol=lf
